= Oolong Tea (烏龍 / 青茶)

_Same species as the other three teas (_Camellia sinensis_); the differentiation is by_ partial oxidation _(10–70%, the broadest oxidation range of the four categories) and by the characteristic_ Fujian / Taiwan production techniques. _Oolong_ postdates the Bencao Gangmu (1578) _as a recognized distinct category — its invention is dated to the late Ming / early Qing, 17th c., in the Wuyi mountains of Fujian — and so it has_ no canonical entry in the early Bencao tradition. _Its classical pharmacy status is_ derivative _from green tea (its less-oxidized end) and red tea (its more-oxidized end), and the formal medical placement is reasoned within the saṃskāra-by-saṃskāra tea framework. Ayurveda: not classical. Greco-Roman: not classical._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Camellia sinensis_ (L.) Kuntze, family Theaceae — same species as green / red / pu-erh tea. The processing distinction is what defines oolong; see *Processing and category definition* below.

*English:* _Oolong tea_ (also _wulong_, _wu long_) — direct transliteration of Mandarin 烏龍 (wūlóng, "black dragon"), via the same southern-Chinese maritime-trade transmission that delivered "tea." A widespread English alternative name: *blue tea* (occasionally; a translation of 青茶 _qīngchá_, "blue/green tea," the older Chinese classification). The "wulong" spelling preserves the Mandarin pronunciation more transparently than "oolong" but the latter is the established English form.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "oolong, n."); the wūlóng / "black dragon" naming has multiple folk etymologies (some say the leaves resemble a dragon's twisted form, some say a black-snake found in the original Fujian tea garden was named for it). The dual category-name 烏龍 / 青茶 — the former more common in trade and the latter more common in formal Chinese pharmacology classification — is standard.]

*Chinese:* 烏龍茶 (wūlóngchá, "black dragon tea") or 青茶 (qīngchá, "blue / green tea" — note that 青 _qīng_ covers the green-blue color range and is used for partial-oxidation teas in formal Chinese tea-pharmacy classification). The formal Chinese six-tea-category system (六大茶類) places the partial-oxidation teas under 青茶, with 烏龍茶 a popular / trade name. Specific famous oolongs:
- *鐵觀音 (Tiěguānyīn, "Iron Bodhisattva of Mercy")* — Anxi county, Fujian; the canonical *low-to-medium oxidation* (~15–30%) oolong; aromatic, floral, with a characteristic orchid / lilac note. Traditionally tightly-rolled; modern "qing xiang" 清香 style is greener and floral, "nong xiang" 濃香 style is roasted and amber.
- *大紅袍 (Dàhóngpáo, "Big Red Robe")* — Wuyi mountains, Fujian; the canonical *high-oxidation, heavily-charcoal-roasted* (~50–70%) Wuyi cliff oolong (武夷岩茶 Wuyi yancha); rich, mineral, with the famous Wuyi "rock-rhyme" 岩韻 yán yùn — a stone-mineral terroir note characteristic to Wuyi.
- *鳳凰單叢 (Fènghuáng Dāncóng, "Phoenix Single Bush")* — Chaozhou, Guangdong; named after the Fenghuang mountain; single-cultivar oolongs from individual ancient trees; remarkable aromatic diversity — single-cultivar oolongs come in many aromatic types (honey orchid, ginger flower, almond, etc.).
- *凍頂烏龍 (Dòngdǐng Wūlóng, "Frozen Peak Oolong")* — Nantou, Taiwan; medium-oxidation; the Taiwanese classical reference oolong.
- *高山烏龍 (Gāoshān Wūlóng, "High Mountain Oolong")* — Taiwan; high-altitude oolongs (above 1000 m: Alishan, Lishan, Shanlinxi); light oxidation, exceptional fragrance.
- *東方美人 (Dōngfāng Měirén, "Oriental Beauty")* / *白毫烏龍 (Báiháo Wūlóng, "White-Tip Oolong")* — Taiwan; *the most oxidized of oolongs* (~70%), naturally infected by tea-leafhopper (_Jacobiasca formosana_), which causes the tea-plant to produce hotrienol and related compounds that give a famous honeyed-grape character.#footnote[Famous oolong identification and processing: Lin, _Bowl of Tea_ (Tuttle, 2008) and standard tea-history literature. The 鐵觀音 qing-xiang / nong-xiang style distinction is industry-standard. The Wuyi yancha "rock-rhyme" 岩韻 (yan yun) concept is canonical to Wuyi connoisseurship. Oriental Beauty / Bai Hao Oolong tea-leafhopper biochemistry: published phytochemistry literature on insect-stress-induced terpene biosynthesis.]

*Sanskrit:* No classical or modern Sanskrit name.

== Processing and category definition

Oolong is *defined by partial oxidation*, made possible by an extended *bruise-and-rest* step that initiates oxidation while a final 殺青 (kill-green) step arrests it at a chosen level. The canonical sequence:
1. *Pluck* (採摘) — mature leaves (3 leaves and a bud is typical, more mature than for green tea).
2. *Solar wither* (曬青) — outdoors in sun; reduces moisture and initiates aroma development.
3. *Indoor wither / bruise* (浪青 / 搖青) — controlled rolling / shaking / bruising that ruptures cell walls and exposes polyphenol oxidase to substrate, initiating *oxidation*. This step may continue over many hours, with alternating bruising and resting; the duration and intensity calibrate the final oxidation level.
4. *Kill green* (殺青) — high-heat pan-firing that denatures the oxidase and *arrests oxidation at the chosen percentage*. (This step is identical in principle to green-tea kill-green; the difference is *when* it is applied relative to the bruise-rest cycle.)
5. *Roll / shape* (揉捻 / 包揉) — Tieguanyin-style tightly-rolled balls or Wuyi-style stripped leaves.
6. *Dry* (烘乾) — moisture stabilization.
7. *Charcoal roast* (炭焙, optional but characteristic for Wuyi yancha and traditional Tieguanyin) — repeated low-heat roasting over hours-to-days; converts polyphenols to roast-modified compounds and amplifies the warming character.

*Pharmacologically*, the partial-oxidation level determines the catechin / theaflavin / theasinensin balance: light oxidation retains most catechins (closer to green); heavy oxidation generates more theaflavins and theasinensins (closer to red tea). The intermediate is its own range.#footnote[Processing technical detail: Chinese-tea-industry literature; cf. Hicks, _The Production of Commercial Tea_ (academic press). The polyphenol-oxidation timing is the foundational chemistry of oolong differentiation; quantification studies on lightly-vs.-heavily-oxidized oolong: published _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_ literature.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_The category postdates the Bencao Gangmu (1578) as a distinct named drug; oolong has no entry under that name in the major Tang–Ming Bencao tradition. The Chinese-medical placement is by extension from the 茶 (chá) entry and by later Qing-period and modern Chinese tea-dietetic literature. The placement below is documented in Qing-era and modern Chinese tea-pharmacology / yangsheng literature, not in the major early Bencao._

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Neutral (平) to slightly warm (微溫) — *intermediate* between green tea (cool) and red tea (warm), exactly as the partial-oxidation degree would suggest. Lightly oxidized oolongs are closer to neutral / cool; heavily oxidized and roasted oolongs (Wuyi yancha, charcoal-roasted Tieguanyin) are closer to warm.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), bitter (苦) — with a pronounced *huígān* 回甘 (returning sweetness) prized in tasting.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺), Kidney (腎); the warming roasted forms add a Liver (肝) note for the qi-moving / appetite-promoting action.

*Actions & indications (classical Chinese tea-dietetic reading):*
- 提神醒腦 — refreshes the spirit and awakens the mind (cognitive support).
- 助消化 / 消食 — *aids digestion / disperses food stagnation* — *the principal modern Chinese functional use*: oolong is the canonical Chinese "after-fatty-meal" tea, with strong cultural association in Fujian / Guangdong / Taiwan, where it accompanies the famous gong-fu cha 工夫茶 small-cup tea service traditional to those regions.
- 化痰 — transforms phlegm (mild; less than green tea's heat-pattern phlegm-clearing).
- 利水 — promotes urination, drains water (mild diuretic, all four teas have this).
- 提氣 — raises qi (the alertness-and-mood-lifting effect).
- *Modern Chinese functional-food restatement:* lipid-lowering / cardiovascular-supportive — the modern oolong-marketing register; clinical evidence modest.

*Cautions:* moderate caffeine; similar to green and red teas; the heavier-roasted forms can be warming-aggravating in heat-pattern conditions; iron-binding tannins similar to green tea; pregnancy caffeine guidance applies.#footnote[The classical Chinese medical placement of oolong as the partial-oxidation intermediate is documented in late-Qing and modern Chinese tea-dietetics literature (e.g. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_; modern Chinese herbal materia medica). The cultural association with gong-fu cha and the after-fatty-meal use are well documented in Chinese tea-culture literature.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry; reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — from the floral / fruity / honeyed notes), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — moderate), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — moderate, less than green tea).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry — less so than green tea due to the partial oxidation introducing modest snigdha-like notes), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp).

*Reasoned vīrya:* *Neutral to slightly warming* — *the standout feature; oolong is the saṃskāra-intermediate tea*. Light oxidation: close to green tea (cool); heavy oxidation + charcoal roast: distinctly warming.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), with sweet (मधुर) for the heavily-roasted / honeyed variants.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha; Vāta-aggravating less than green tea (the partial oxidation moderates the laghu-rūkṣa); Pitta-neutral to mildly aggravating in heavy-roast forms. Suitable in Vāta + Kapha mixed states more than green tea.

*Reasoned karma:* _Medhya_, _kaphahara_, _dīpana / pācana_ (notably stronger than green tea — the canonical post-meal digestive use), _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_, _hṛd-rakṣaka_, _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_; in excess, _nidrā-nāśana_, _rasa-kṣaya_. The *digestive register* is more pronounced than green tea, consistent with the partial oxidation.#footnote[Reasoned placement closely parallels the modern Indian / Western functional-food framing of oolong as "digestive / metabolic" tea; the post-meal Fujianese tradition is exactly this register. Flagged interpretive.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(F) Chinese folk:* same gut-rules as green tea — don't drink on empty stomach (less of a tea-drunkenness risk than green tea, but still real); the gong-fu cha tradition uses small concentrated cups, which is *more* tannic and more iron-binding than typical Western steeping.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* ~30–60 mg per cup (typical Western steeping); the gong-fu cha small-cup tradition can yield 70–100 mg per concentrated session — equivalent to coffee.
  - *Iron absorption:* tannin-iron binding similar to green tea.
  - *Drug interactions:* similar to green tea; less concentrated EGCG (most has been oxidized to theasinensins / theaflavins).
  - *Pregnancy:* limit caffeine.
  - *Heavy charcoal-roast forms:* may contain trace polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) from the smoke contact; verify against current sourcing and processing literature; clinical relevance modest at normal consumption.
  - *Aluminium:* tea leaves accumulate aluminium; clinical relevance modest at normal consumption.#footnote[Charcoal-roasted oolong PAH content: published phytochemistry of roast-processed teas; verify against current literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Oolong is *not classical in Tibb* — see _green-tea.typ_ for the broader tea-and-Tibb framework. Tea entered the Persian-Arabic-Ottoman world only in the 17th–19th c., far postdating the medieval Tibb canon; oolong specifically — as a Fujian-Taiwanese partial-oxidation category invented in the 17th–18th c. — is *post-classical-of-the-post-classical*, with essentially no Tibb medieval pharmacology. Reasoned modern Unani placement on the tea axis would treat oolong as *Hot-Neutral and Dry* in the 1st degree — milder warming than red tea and milder cooling than green, mirroring the Chinese intermediate-oxidation placement. The substance is not in the Persian Tibb-e Sonnati corpus and has no Avicennan attestation; modern usage is by extension on the tea axis.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

The formal-pharmacy literature treats oolong principally as a *daily dietetic / functional drink*, not as a compound-formula ingredient. The cultural-medical preparation tradition is *gōngfu chá* (工夫茶, "skill-tea" / "kung-fu tea"), the small-cup, short-steep, high-leaf-ratio brewing tradition of Fujian / Guangdong / Taiwan that is the *canonical oolong preparation*. Each steep is short (10–60 seconds), with up to 10–15 successive steeps from a single leaf charge, each revealing different aspects of the leaf as the oxidation/roast profile unfolds in solution.

- *Gong-fu chá preparation:* small clay pot (purple-clay yixing _zǐshāhú_ 紫砂壺 is canonical); 5–10 g of leaf per 100–150 mL pot; quick rinse-and-pour; short successive steeps. The preparation itself is treated as a meditative-medicinal practice (the Chinese tea-ceremony tradition).
- *鐵觀音 traditional preparation*; *Wuyi yancha gōngfu*; *Taiwanese gong-fu*.
- *Modern bottled functional drinks:* the Japanese / Taiwanese bottled oolong industry has become a major commercial category; clinical evidence for the marketed "fat-burning" function is *very weak* — flagged.#footnote[Gong-fu cha: Heiss & Heiss, _The Story of Tea_ (Ten Speed Press, 2007); Chinese tea-culture literature. The yixing pot tradition is canonical to gong-fu cha. The "fat-burning oolong" marketing claim is dominantly Japanese-marketing-driven and is at substantial variance with rigorous clinical evidence.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical formulas. Modern Indian / Western functional-food use only.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical European tradition; oolong was a late and small fraction of European tea consumption (the British Empire favored fully-oxidized black / red tea). Modern Western consumption is principally Japanese / Taiwanese-export bottled drinks and specialty tea-shop loose-leaf — recent and culturally rather than pharmacologically grounded.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Caffeine:* ~2–3% of dry leaf; similar to green and red teas.
- *Catechins:* substantial (~10–20% of dry leaf — less than green tea, more than red tea), with EGCG present but reduced.
- *Theaflavins and theasinensins:* formed during partial oxidation; theasinensins are *characteristic of oolong* and are the polyphenol class that distinguishes its chemistry from green and red teas.
- *L-theanine:* substantial (similar to green tea).
- *Volatile aromatics:* particularly developed by the bruise-and-rest process and the optional charcoal roast — methyl jasmonate, ionones, linalool, geraniol, benzyl alcohol, jasmin lactone, indole, and many others; *oolongs are the most aromatic of all teas*, and the aromatic diversity is part of their connoisseur prestige.
- *Charcoal-roast modifications:* furans, pyrazines, Maillard reaction products from sugar / amino-acid heating.#footnote[Oolong chemistry: reviews in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_ and _Food Chemistry_; theasinensins characterization (Tanaka et al.).]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Caffeine:* applies fully.
- *L-theanine + caffeine:* the calm-alert synergy applies.
- *Theasinensins:* preclinical evidence for cardiovascular and metabolic effects; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Lipid-lowering / "fat-burning":* small clinical trials report modest reductions in triglycerides and modest improvements in lipid profile with regular oolong consumption; the popular "fat-burning oolong" marketing claim is *substantially overstated* in commercial literature.
- *Antioxidant:* substantial; the partial oxidation introduces theaflavins / theasinensins that have their own (different from EGCG) antioxidant profile.
- *Cardiovascular:* modest evidence for blood-pressure / lipid improvements with regular consumption.
- *Cognitive / mood:* L-theanine + caffeine combined effects.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical Chinese after-meal digestive use is the most defensible health register; the modern "fat-burning" claim is the principal exaggeration to flag.#footnote[Clinical evidence: small studies and meta-analyses in _Journal of Nutritional Biochemistry_, _European Journal of Clinical Nutrition_; verify against current systematic reviews. The "fat-burning" claim is at substantial variance with rigorous clinical evidence — flagged per project rule.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Brewed oolong tea (Western style):* 3–5 cups/day; well-tolerated.
- *Gong-fu cha (concentrated):* a single session uses ~5–10 g of leaf; multiple steeps deliver ~70–150 mg caffeine; respect caffeine guidance.
- *Same risk groups as green tea:* iron-deficiency anaemia (separate tea from iron-rich meals), pregnancy (limit caffeine), arrhythmia, insomnia, anxiety disorder, peptic ulcer.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Reference thresholds; not personalized medical advice. The gong-fu cha tradition's concentrated brewing produces higher per-session caffeine and tannin than typical Western steeping; relevant practical point.]

